{
  "comprehensible": "The board dealt with the proposal quickly and without much attention, and the CFO was left to take the blame.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "gave the proposal short shrift",
      "gloss": "dealt with the proposal quickly and without giving it much attention or consideration",
      "example": "The manager gave my idea short shrift and moved on to the next topic."
    },
    {
      "expression": "carry the can",
      "gloss": "take the blame or responsibility for something that went wrong",
      "example": "After the project failed, the team leader had to carry the can."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": null,
  "translation": "董事会草草处理了该提案，首席财务官只好背黑锅。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "At the last minute, he lost his nerve and did something that harmed his colleagues, even though they trusted him.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "bottled it",
      "gloss": "lost your courage and failed to do something you were supposed to do",
      "example": "He was going to ask for a raise, but he bottled it and said nothing."
    },
    {
      "expression": "threw his colleagues under the bus",
      "gloss": "did something that hurt your friends or coworkers to protect yourself",
      "example": "When the boss asked who made the mistake, she threw her team under the bus and said it was their fault."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": null,
  "translation": "他在最后一刻临阵退缩，还出卖了同事。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The manager did not admit that the numbers had been changed in a dishonest way until after the auditors had left.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "doctored",
      "gloss": "changed in a dishonest way to trick people",
      "example": "He doctored the photo to make it look like he was on holiday, but his friends knew he had stayed home."
    },
    {
      "expression": "auditors",
      "gloss": "people whose job is to check financial records",
      "example": "The auditors looked at all the company's accounts to make sure everything was correct."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The sentence begins with 'Not until...', which makes the main clause use inverted word order: 'did the manager admit' instead of 'the manager admitted'. This structure emphasizes that the admission happened only after the auditors left.",
  "translation": "直到审计员离开后，经理才承认数据被篡改了。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The house was in a state of polite, slightly shabby decay. Its residents had moved to the Continent a long time ago.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "genteel decay",
      "gloss": "a kind of decay that is polite or upper-class, not rough or dirty",
      "example": "The old hotel showed genteel decay: the paint was faded, but the furniture was still elegant."
    },
    {
      "expression": "repaired to",
      "gloss": "went to; moved to (a formal way to say 'went')",
      "example": "After the party, the guests repaired to the garden for coffee."
    },
    {
      "expression": "the Continent",
      "gloss": "mainland Europe (often used by British people)",
      "example": "They sold their house in London and moved to the Continent."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The phrase 'its occupants having long since repaired to the Continent' is a participle clause. It gives a reason or background for the main clause. It means 'because its occupants had moved to the Continent a long time before'. The word 'having' shows the action was completed before the main situation.",
  "translation": "这栋房子处于一种优雅而破败的状态，因为它的住户早已移居到欧洲大陆去了。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The results hint at something but do not prove it, and people should not read too much into them.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "suggestive rather than conclusive",
      "gloss": "hinting at something but not proving it",
      "example": "Her smile was suggestive rather than conclusive; it might have meant she was happy, or it might have meant nothing."
    },
    {
      "expression": "over-read",
      "gloss": "to understand or interpret something as having more meaning or importance than it really has",
      "example": "Don't over-read his short email; he was just busy, not angry."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": null,
  "translation": "这些发现只是提示性的，而非结论性的，不应当被过度解读。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The person who rents the property must protect the owner from any claims that come from breaking any of the promises in this agreement.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "indemnify",
      "gloss": "to protect someone from loss or damage by paying for it",
      "example": "The company will indemnify you if your phone is stolen while you are at work."
    },
    {
      "expression": "lessee",
      "gloss": "the person who rents something",
      "example": "The lessee must pay the rent on the first day of each month."
    },
    {
      "expression": "lessor",
      "gloss": "the person who owns something and rents it to someone else",
      "example": "The lessor will fix the broken heater."
    },
    {
      "expression": "covenants",
      "gloss": "formal promises in a legal agreement",
      "example": "The contract has several covenants, such as keeping the garden tidy."
    },
    {
      "expression": "breach",
      "gloss": "an act of breaking a law or agreement",
      "example": "Not paying the rent is a breach of the contract."
    },
    {
      "expression": "claims",
      "gloss": "requests for money or demands for your rights",
      "example": "The insurance company received many claims after the storm."
    },
    {
      "expression": "arising from",
      "gloss": "coming from or caused by",
      "example": "The argument arising from the mistake made everyone upset."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The sentence uses legal language and passive-like structure. 'The lessee shall indemnify the lessor against all claims' means the lessee must protect the lessor from all claims. 'Arising from any breach of the covenants herein' tells you what kind of claims: those that come from breaking any of the promises in this document. The word 'herein' means 'in this document'.",
  "translation": "承租人应赔偿出租人因违反本协议中的任何约定而引起的所有索赔。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The report does not go as far as saying who is to blame, but it is very easy to see what it suggests.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "stops short of",
      "gloss": "does not go as far as; does not do something completely",
      "example": "She stopped short of calling him a liar, but everyone understood what she meant."
    },
    {
      "expression": "apportioning blame",
      "gloss": "saying who is responsible for something bad",
      "example": "The teacher did not apportion blame for the broken window; she just asked what happened."
    },
    {
      "expression": "hard to miss",
      "gloss": "very easy to notice or understand",
      "example": "The sign was huge, so it was hard to miss."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": null,
  "translation": "报告没有直接归咎于任何人，但其暗示显而易见。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "He was very unhappy with the result, to say the least.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "to put it mildly",
      "gloss": "used to say that you are describing something in a gentle way, when the truth is much stronger",
      "example": "The food was bad, to put it mildly; I couldn't eat it."
    },
    {
      "expression": "not best pleased",
      "gloss": "very annoyed or unhappy",
      "example": "She was not best pleased when I forgot her birthday."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": null,
  "translation": "说得客气一点，他对这个结果很不高兴。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The committee thought it was just a normal mistake, but it was actually done on purpose. It was meant to save the minister from having to answer a difficult question.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "taken for",
      "gloss": "thought to be; believed to be",
      "example": "She took the stranger for a thief, but he was just a lost tourist."
    },
    {
      "expression": "routine oversight",
      "gloss": "a usual mistake that happens because someone forgot to do something",
      "example": "The missing signature was a routine oversight, not a serious problem."
    },
    {
      "expression": "deliberate omission",
      "gloss": "something left out on purpose",
      "example": "Leaving his name off the list was a deliberate omission; she did it to hurt him."
    },
    {
      "expression": "spare",
      "gloss": "save someone from something unpleasant",
      "example": "He told a small lie to spare her feelings."
    },
    {
      "expression": "awkward question",
      "gloss": "a difficult or embarrassing question",
      "example": "The reporter asked an awkward question about the money."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The sentence has a contrast: 'What the committee had taken for a routine oversight' is the subject, and 'was in fact a deliberate omission' is the main verb. The phrase 'designed to spare...' describes the omission. The structure is not too hard, but the contrast between 'routine oversight' and 'deliberate omission' is important.",
  "translation": "委员会原以为那只是一次普通的疏忽，但实际上那是故意省略的，目的是让部长免于回答一个尴尬的问题。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The company was responsible for paying an amount of money that it could not easily pay.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "on the hook for",
      "gloss": "responsible for paying or dealing with something",
      "example": "After the car accident, she was on the hook for the repair costs."
    },
    {
      "expression": "a sum it could ill afford",
      "gloss": "an amount of money that it could not easily pay",
      "example": "They bought a house that they could ill afford, and soon they had money problems."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": null,
  "translation": "公司不得不承担一笔它难以负担的款项。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "The reaction happens only when a catalyst is there. If there is no catalyst, the activation energy is so high that the reaction cannot happen.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "proceeds",
      "gloss": "happens or goes forward",
      "example": "The meeting proceeds even if the manager is late."
    },
    {
      "expression": "in the presence of",
      "gloss": "when something is there",
      "example": "Plants grow well in the presence of sunlight."
    },
    {
      "expression": "catalyst",
      "gloss": "a substance that makes a chemical reaction go faster without being used up itself",
      "example": "A catalyst helps the reaction start, but it is not part of the final product."
    },
    {
      "expression": "without which",
      "gloss": "if it is not there",
      "example": "You need a key, without which you cannot open the door."
    },
    {
      "expression": "activation energy",
      "gloss": "the energy needed to start a chemical reaction",
      "example": "The activation energy for this reaction is low, so it starts easily."
    },
    {
      "expression": "prohibitively high",
      "gloss": "so high that it stops something from happening",
      "example": "The cost of the car was prohibitively high, so we did not buy it."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The phrase 'without which' introduces a condition: it means 'if there is no catalyst'. The main clause says the reaction happens only with a catalyst; the 'without which' clause explains what happens without it. The word 'prohibitively' modifies 'high' and means 'so high that it prevents the reaction'.",
  "translation": "该反应只有在催化剂存在时才能进行；如果没有催化剂，活化能会高得使反应无法发生。"
}

{
  "comprehensible": "It is right for us to think about whether this way of doing things agrees with what we must do.",
  "glosses": [
    {
      "expression": "behoves",
      "gloss": "is right or proper for (someone) to do",
      "example": "It behoves you to say thank you when someone helps you."
    },
    {
      "expression": "course of action",
      "gloss": "a plan or way of doing something",
      "example": "The teacher chose a different course of action to help the students learn."
    },
    {
      "expression": "consonant with",
      "gloss": "in agreement with; matching",
      "example": "His actions are consonant with his words, so I trust him."
    }
  ],
  "grammar": "The sentence uses 'It behoves us to consider...' where 'It' is a placeholder subject and the real subject is 'us'. This is a formal way to say 'We should consider...'. Also, 'whether such a course of action is consonant with our obligations' is a long noun clause that acts as the object of 'consider'.",
  "translation": "我们应当考虑这样的做法是否符合我们的义务。"
}
